    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f4817fa1-7160-5d08-adc7-564c3155c399",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $N$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $N$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(N)=0$\n\n$$\nN=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(N) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & 1 & 2 \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{1}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2 & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+(-1)|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & - 4 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}|+2 \\sqrt{2}|\\begin{aligned}- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} & - \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ - \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3} & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2})+(-1)(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{3})+2 \\sqrt{2}(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}- \\frac{4 \\sqrt{2}}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}\\cdot \\frac{10 \\sqrt{2}}{3}+(-1)\\cdot \\frac{16}{3}+2 \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{9}- \\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(N)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true.",
